Data is not static because business is not static. 
Schema Evolution is the automated ability of a modern data system
to gracefully handle changes to the structure of incoming data—like a 
new column being added or a data type changing—without breaking downstream pipelines or requiring a massive historical rewrite.

Technical Breakdown :- 
Historically, if an upstream application added a new discount_code column to its JSON payload, 
a rigid ETL pipeline would throw a SchemaMismatchException and crash instantly. 
With modern Lakehouse formats like Delta Lake or Apache Iceberg, schema evolution is
handled at the metadata layer. When writing via Spark, if you pass the mergeSchema=true option, 
the execution engine detects the new column, automatically issues the equivalent of an ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN operation 
to the underlying catalog, and writes the new data. For all older, 
historical files that don't possess this column, the query engine simply returns a NULL at read time, ensuring perfect backward compatibility.

Buisness Context :-
Product engineering teams ship new features daily, constantly altering the telemetry payloads generated by mobile apps and web services.
If the data platform cannot evolve its schema automatically, the data engineering team becomes a massive operational bottleneck, 
spending 40% of their sprints executing manual DDL scripts while business analysts wait days just to query the new product metrics.

Design Decision :-
The biggest mistake I see junior engineers make is turning on automated schema evolution globally for all tables,
treating it as a magic bullet. This inevitably turns a data lake into a data swamp; an upstream bug might accidentally cast revenue from a Float to a String,
which Airflow will happily ingest, silently destroying every BI dashboard downstream. 
As a data engineer, I enforce strict Schema Contracts. Safe additions (new columns) are allowed to evolve automatically,
but destructive mutations (dropping columns, altering data types) instantly trip a circuit breaker, halting the pipeline for manual review.

